<?xml version="1.0"?>
<clickhouse>
  <!--
    Docker mem_limit for clickhouse service = 3072m (3 GiB).
    Internal cap set to 2.5 GiB (2684354560 bytes) to leave ~512 MiB headroom
    for OS page cache and Docker overhead.

    History:
      Originally 800 MB — too low once system logs accumulated.
      Raised 2026-04-29 to 3.5 GiB cap when docker limit was 4096m.
      Reduced 2026-07-05: docker limit changed to 1536m (cap/comment were stale).
      Root cause of OOM death spiral: 3002+ fragmented parts in task_events table
      required > 1.5 GiB RSS at startup; container was OOM-killed 2344 times.
      Fix: raised docker limit to 3072m, cap to 2.5 GiB; truncated system logs;
      background merge will reduce part count over 2-3 days.
  -->
  <max_server_memory_usage>2684354560</max_server_memory_usage>

  <!--
    REMOVED (was here until 2026-07-05, absence confirmed deliberate 2026-08-15):
      <profiles><default><allow_experimental_json_type>1</allow_experimental_json_type>

    It existed because v4 Goose migrations (task_runs_v1 etc.) use JSON columns and
    ClickHouse 24.x gated that type behind an experimental flag. On the pinned
    25.7.x line the setting reports value=1, changed=0, tier=Production — i.e. it is
    the built-in default and no longer experimental. Verified on 25.7.5 by creating a
    JSON column successfully and by the fact that trigger_dev.task_runs_v2 /
    task_events_v2 / metrics_v1 already store JSON columns and work.

    Do not re-add it: it is dead configuration on the pinned version. If ClickHouse is
    ever downgraded below 25.x (AGENTS.md forbids this), restore it first.
  -->

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       2026-08-15 — SYSTEM LOG VOLUME AT SOURCE (not just retention)

       Incident: the container sat at 100% of its 1-CPU limit indefinitely,
       merging its own telemetry. EU load stayed ~5 with 3 GiB of swap in use,
       which starved every other process on the host — including Uptime Kuma,
       whose outbound checks then timed out. Every EU-originated monitor
       (SG services + client sites) flapped together while SG itself was
       provably healthy: ICMP EU->SG showed 0% loss, but TCP :80 failed 11/20;
       the same test from a laptop passed 20/20.

       Measured split at the time of diagnosis:
         system      16.89 GiB   643,628,136 rows   <- ClickHouse's own telemetry
         trigger_dev 49.00 MiB       624,481 rows   <- the actual application data
       i.e. ~345x more self-telemetry than real data, and trace_log alone was
       ingesting 1,240,456 rows/hour.

       Why the 2026-07-04 fix below (TTL) did not hold: TTL bounds how long rows
       are KEPT, not how fast they are PRODUCED. Verified still working at
       diagnosis time (trace_log spanned 10 days against a 7-day TTL, text_log
       exactly 14). The merge load is driven by the production rate, so 7 days of
       a 1.24M rows/hour firehose is still 200M+ rows to merge continuously.
       Retention tuning cannot fix a generation-rate problem — the generators
       themselves have to be turned off or slowed down. That is what follows.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- trace_log: the sampling profiler. 10.25 GiB / 446M rows, 1.24M rows/hour,
       and never queried by anyone here. Removing the config disables the trace
       collector outright, which is the documented way to switch it off. Re-enable
       temporarily (delete this line, restart) only for a deliberate perf
       investigation, and turn it back off afterwards. -->
  <trace_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- asynchronous_metric_log: 99.5M rows, and its event_date range started at
       1970-01-01, so part pruning could not work on it either. Host-level metrics
       are already collected by Netdata + Grafana, so this table is pure overhead. -->
  <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1"/>

  <!-- metric_log: kept — it is the only ClickHouse-internal metric source — but
       the default 7.5s flush created one part per flush, ~11.5k parts/day, which
       is what produced the 402 active parts and the permanent merge backlog.
       At 60s the part rate drops ~8x while the metrics stay usable. -->
  <metric_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    <flush_interval_milliseconds>60000</flush_interval_milliseconds>
  </metric_log>

  <!-- text_log: 5.33 GiB / 90M rows in 14 days = ~6.4M rows/day, because it
       mirrors EVERY server log line into a table. Container logs already go to
       Docker's json driver, so this exists only for in-SQL post-incident search.
       Restricting it to warning-and-above keeps that value and removes the flood;
       3 days is enough for the incident window it is actually used in. -->
  <text_log>
    <level>warning</level>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    <flush_interval_milliseconds>30000</flush_interval_milliseconds>
  </text_log>

  <!-- ── System log TTL: tiered retention policy ──────────────────────────
       Rationale:
         7d  — high-volume traces/metrics (replaced by Netdata for long-term)
         14d — query & text logs (useful for post-incident perf regression)
         30d — error log (recurring / rare bugs need a month of history)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <processors_profile_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </processors_profile_log>
  <latency_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </latency_log>
  <query_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </query_log>
  <error_log>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </error_log>
</clickhouse>
